--
N5008 [dcl.typedef]/2: `using F = void(int);' declares the same type as
`typedef void F(int);'.  With the alias, `F *p = &cb;' violated the
invariant "symbol type must match" (cpp_typecheck_initializer.cpp), and
assigning &cb to a member of type F * was a conversion error (bin53): the
parser keeps the type-id's parameters in parsed form (cpp_declaration) and
records its noexcept as `noexcept' rather than `#C_noexcept'.  The typedef
symbol's function type is now converted like a declarator's.
test_member.desc: member alias-declarations (libc++'s
__stop_callback_base declares `using __callback_fn_t =
void(__stop_callback_base*) noexcept;', and the body of
__stop_callback_base::__invoke was dropped, __stop_token/stop_state.h:33).
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
